>> MF=3 NEUTRON CROSS SECTIONS                                    9222 1451  215
                                                                  9222 1451  216
              ----- GENERAL INFORMATION -----                     9222 1451  217
                                                                  9222 1451  218
      EVALUATION ABOVE THE UNRESOLVED RESONANCE REGION            9222 1451  219
            P. G. Young and M. B. Chadwick (LANL)                 9222 1451  220
                    January, 2003                                 9222 1451  221
   The maximum energy of the evaluation (Yo03) was increased to   9222 1451  222
30 MeV. Above the unresolved resonance region (En=40 keV), new    9222 1451  223
evaluations were performed of the neutron total, elastic, (n,n'), 9222 1451  224
(n,2n), (n,3n), (n,4n), (n,f), (n,nf), (n,2nf), (n,3nf), (n,4nf), 9222 1451  225
and (n,gamma) cross sections, as well as their associated angular 9222 1451  226
and energy distributions. The energy range of the evaluation was  9222 1451  227
increased from 20 to 30 MeV.                                      9222 1451  228
   To provide the new data, coupled channel optical model calcu-  9222 1451  229
lations were performed with the ECIS96 code for the lowest 7      9222 1451  230
members of the U233 ground state rotational band. These calcu-    9222 1451  231
lations were used to provide elastic and inelastic cross sections 9222 1451  232
and angular distributions, and to provide neutron transmission    9222 1451  233
coefficients for nuclear reaction theory calculations with the    9222 1451  234
GNASH (Ch01,Yo98) Hauser- Feshbach statistical/fission/           9222 1451  235
preequilibrium code. The GNASH calculations were used directly    9222 1451  236
for the correlated energy-angle spectra in MF=6 for the           9222 1451  237
(n,n'continuum), (n,2n), (n,3n), and (n,4n) reactions.            9222 1451  238
   The total cross section (MT=1), the radiative capture cross    9222 1451  239
section (MT=102) and the fission cross section (MT=18) are based  9222 1451  240
all or in part on experimental data.  Experimental data were      9222 1451  241
obtained mainly from the EXFOR data base.  All other cross        9222 1451  242
sections are based on theoretical calculations.  In addition, the 9222 1451  243
shape of the radiative capture cross section at lower energies    9222 1451  244
and the absolute values above 1 MeV are from the GNASH analysis.  9222 1451  245
                                                                  9222 1451  246
                                                                  9222 1451  247
            ----- DETAILS OF LANL REVISION -----                  9222 1451  248
                                                                  9222 1451  249
MT=1: TOTAL CROSS SECTION                                         9222 1451  250
   The ENDF/B-VI evaluation of the total cross section (MT=1),    9222 1451  251
based mainly on experimental data but especially [Fo71], was      9222 1451  252
adopted over the energy range 1-15 MeV.  The ENDF/B-VI results    9222 1451  253
were modified slightly below 1 MeV to better conform with         9222 1451  254
experimental data. The optical model analysis (Yo03) was used for 9222 1451  255
the cross section from 15 to 30 MeV.                              9222 1451  256
                                                                  9222 1451  257
MT=2: ELASTIC SCATTERING CROSS SECTION                            9222 1451  258
   The elastic cross section is obtained by subtracting the       9222 1451  259
nonelastic cross section (MT=3) from the total cross section      9222 1451  260
(MT=1).                                                           9222 1451  261
                                                                  9222 1451  262
MT=3: NONELASTIC CROSS SECTION                                    9222 1451  263
   The (redundant) nonelastic cross section is the sum of the     9222 1451  264
following reactions: MT=4,16,17,18,37,102.                        9222 1451  265
                                                                  9222 1451  266
MT=4: INELASTIC CROSS SECTION                                     9222 1451  267
   Sum of MT=51-91.                                               9222 1451  268
                                                                  9222 1451  269
MT=16,17,37: (n,xn) CROSS SECTIONS                                9222 1451  270
   The (n,xn) cross sections are based on an analysis with the    9222 1451  271
GNASH nuclear model code (Yo98). The (n,5n) cross section becomes 9222 1451  272
significant above about 25 MeV.  Because there is no ENDF-6 MT    9222 1451  273
number for this reaction, we have arbitrarily included this cross 9222 1451  274
section in the (n,4n) reaction.  Consequently, the (n,4n) cross   9222 1451  275
section becomes unphysical above about 25 MeV.                    9222 1451  276
                                                                  9222 1451  277
MT=18: FISSION NEUTRON CROSS SECTION                              9222 1451  278
   Much of the 233U fission cross section data are in the form of 9222 1451  279
ratios relative to 235U. All 233U(n,f) ratio and cross section    9222 1451  280
data were converted to ENDF/B-VII standards [see Pronyaev et al.  9222 1451  281
(Pr05)], and a covariance analysis of the data was performed. The 9222 1451  282
results were smoothed and used for the (n,f) cross section, after 9222 1451  283
small modifications. The somewhat higher 235U fission cross       9222 1451  284
section in the fission spectrum region produces better agreement  9222 1451  285
with with fast critical benchmark experiments.  Important         9222 1451  286
experimental data included in the ENDF/B-VII analysis include the 9222 1451  287
data of Li02, Me74, Me88, and Ka86.                               9222 1451  288
                                                                  9222 1451  289
MT=19,20,21,38: MULTI-CHANCE FISSION CROSS SECTIONS               9222 1451  290
  The ratios of the multi-chance fission cross sections to the    9222 1451  291
total fission cross section were obtained from GNASH              9222 1451  292
calculations.  The evaluated multi-chance fission cross sections  9222 1451  293
were then obtained by multiplying the MT=18 total fission cross   9222 1451  294
section by the ratios.                                            9222 1451  295
                                                                  9222 1451  296
MT=51-53,55,58,65:  DISCRETE INELASTIC CROSS SECTIONS TO GROUND-  9222 1451  297
                    STATE ROTATIONAL BAND LEVELS                  9222 1451  298
   Coupled channel optical model calculations were performed with 9222 1451  299
the ECIS96 code for the lowest 7 members of the U233 ground state 9222 1451  300
rotational band (MT=2,51,52,53,55,58,65), including compound      9222 1451  301
nucleus contributions. These calculations were also used to       9222 1451  302
provide neutron transmission coefficients for nuclear reaction    9222 1451  303
theory calculations with the GNASH (Yo98) and COMNUC (Du71)       9222 1451  304
Hauser- Feshbach statistical-fission-preequilibrium codes.  See   9222 1451  305
description above under GENERAL INFORMATION.                      9222 1451  306
                                                                  9222 1451  307
MT=54,56-57,59-64,66-88: DISCRETE INELASTIC NEUTRON CROSS         9222 1451  308
                         SECTIONS                                 9222 1451  309
   Inelastic cross sections from compound-nucleus reactions were  9222 1451  310
calculated with ECIS96 for all states. In addition, ECIS96 was    9222 1451  311
used for vibrational model calculations for 3- and 2+ states in   9222 1451  312
238U which were then included in lumped form in the 233U analysis 9222 1451  313
(MT=84-88).                                                       9222 1451  314
                                                                  9222 1451  315
MT=91:  INELASTIC CONTINUUM NEUTRON CROSS SECTION                 9222 1451  316
   Based on the GNASH Hauser-Feshbach statistical-preequilibrium  9222 1451  317
calculations, described above. Note that MT=91 thresholds at 0.70 9222 1451  318
MeV.  Therefore, discrete states with excitation energies above   9222 1451  319
0.70 MeV (MT=83-88) lie in the MT=91 continuum region.            9222 1451  320
                                                                  9222 1451  321
MT=102: NEUTRON RADIATIVE CAPTURE CROSS SECTION                   9222 1451  322
   1.0-5 to 40 keV:  Based on the resonance parameter evaluation  9222 1451  323
by Leal et al. (see above).                                       9222 1451  324
   40 keV to 30 MeV: The GNASH code system was utilized to        9222 1451  325
calculate the (n,gamma) cross section from 1 keV to 16 MeV. The   9222 1451  326
gamma-ray strength function and level densities were adjusted so  9222 1451  327
that the calculation reproduced the experimental data of Hopkins  9222 1451  328
and Diven (Ho62). The results were used from 0.04 to 16 MeV for   9222 1451  329
the (n,gamma) cross section, with a smooth extrapolation to 30    9222 1451  330
MeV.                                                              9222 1451  331
